              <a href="#outlets" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>14-50 and 6-50 Outlets</span></a>
              <a href="#eighty-percent" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>The 80% Rule</span></a>
              <a href="#connectors" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>J1772, NACS and Adapters</span></a>
              <a href="#listing" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>UL and ETL Listing</span></a>
              <a href="#power-stations" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Why a Power Station Won't Do It</span></a>
              <a href="#portable-vs-wall" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Portable or Plug-In Wall Unit</span></a>
              <a href="#which-one" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Which One to Buy</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          People shop for a portable EV charger by amps and cable length. The thing that actually sets your charging speed, and your safety, is the receptacle on the wall and the circuit behind it. Get that right first and almost any listed charger in this guide will do the job.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="levels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Level 1 vs Level 2: What Each Actually Delivers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The box most people call an EV charger is, technically, electric vehicle supply equipment, or EVSE. The actual charger is inside the car. The EVSE's job is to check that the connection is safe, tell the car how much current it may draw, and switch the power on only once the plug is locked in. That is why a portable EV charger can be a fairly small box on a cord: it is a safety switch and a messenger, not a power plant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Level 1 means 120 volts from an ordinary household outlet. Most portable Level 1 cords draw 12 amps, which is about 1.4 kilowatts and typically adds something like 3 to 5 miles of range per hour, depending on the car. Plug in at 6 p.m. and unplug at 7 a.m., and you might add 40 to 60 miles. For a short commute and a car that sits all night, that is genuinely enough, and the cord that came with many EVs already does it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Level 2 means 240 volts, the same supply as an electric dryer or range. A portable Level 2 charger on a 50-amp circuit typically draws 32 amps, about 7.7 kilowatts, and a plug-in wall unit set to 40 amps gets you to roughly 9.6 kilowatts. Depending on the car, that is somewhere around 20 to 35 miles of range per hour, enough to refill most daily driving in a few hours and a nearly empty battery overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your car sets a ceiling too. Every EV has an onboard charger with a maximum AC rate, and many are rated somewhere around 7 to 11 kilowatts. If your car's onboard charger tops out near 7 kilowatts, a 40-amp unit will not charge it any faster than a 32-amp one. Check the AC charging rate in the owner's manual before paying for amps your car cannot use.
          </p>

          <p>
            DC fast charging, the kind at highway stations, is a different thing altogether. It bypasses the onboard charger and feeds the battery directly at tens or hundreds of kilowatts. Nothing in this guide does that, and nothing you can install in a garage on household service will. A portable EV charger is for overnight and destination charging, which is where most EV owners do most of their charging anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Comparison Table
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three of these picks are true portable cords that you can coil up and put in the trunk. Two are plug-in wall units that hang on a bracket but unplug from a NEMA receptacle, so they can move house with you. The table is built from each maker's published specifications.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Model</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Max current</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Plug</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Connector</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tesla Mobile Connector</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable cord</td>
