        <div id="outlets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. NEMA 14-50 and 6-50 Outlets: Have an Electrician Install Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every portable Level 2 charger is built around the NEMA 14-50 receptacle: a four-prong, 50-amp, 240-volt outlet that also provides a neutral. It is the same receptacle used at RV park pedestals and for many electric ranges, which is why it became the default for EV cords. The NEMA 6-50 is its three-prong sibling, with two hots and a ground but no neutral. EV chargers do not need the neutral, so many plug-in units are offered with either plug.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installing either one is a job for a licensed electrician, and in most places it needs a permit and an inspection. The electrician will check that your panel has capacity for a large continuous load, run the correct wire size for the breaker and the distance, and fit the receptacle in a sensible spot for the cable to reach the charge port. Many will perform a load calculation first; if your service is already near its limit, a lower-amp circuit or a load-management device may be the answer. A <a href="/best-home-energy-monitor/">home energy monitor</a> is a cheap way to see how close to that limit your house already runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask for an industrial or heavy-duty grade receptacle. EV charging pulls high current for hours at a time, every night, and that is a much harder job than a range or dryer that cycles on and off. Inexpensive receptacles and loose terminal screws have been linked to overheated and melted outlets under continuous EV loads, and Tesla, among others, recommends a high-quality receptacle for its Mobile Connector. The price difference is small compared with the cost of a scorched wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ground-fault protection is the other conversation to have up front. Recent editions of the National Electrical Code require GFCI protection for receptacles used for EV charging and for many 240-volt receptacles in garages and outdoors. Most EV chargers include their own ground-fault detection, and some can nuisance-trip when paired with a GFCI breaker. Your electrician and local inspector will know what your jurisdiction requires; if nuisance tripping becomes a problem, hardwiring a wall unit is often the cleanest fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things not to do. Do not plug a portable EV charger into an extension cord; nearly every maker prohibits it, because a long cord and its plug connections become the weakest, hottest point in the circuit. And do not use a homemade or off-brand adapter to plug a 14-50 charger into a smaller dryer receptacle. If you want to use an existing 30-amp dryer outlet, use the maker's own adapter that tells the charger to limit itself to 24 amps, and never run the dryer and the car on the same circuit at once. Our <a href="/best-dryer-cord/">dryer cord guide</a> explains the 10-30 and 14-30 receptacle types.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="eighty-percent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The 80% Continuous Rule and Amp Settings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electrical code treats EV charging as a continuous load, meaning one that runs three hours or more. A continuous load may only use 80 percent of the circuit's rating, or put the other way, the circuit must be sized at 125 percent of the charger's maximum current. That single rule explains every common EV charger rating on the market.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Circuit breaker</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Maximum continuous charging current</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. power at 240V</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Common receptacles</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">15A (120V)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.4 kW at 120V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NEMA 5-15 (Level 1)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20A (120V)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.9 kW at 120V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NEMA 5-20 (Level 1)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.8 kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NEMA 14-30, 10-30</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">40A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">32A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7.7 kW</td>
