                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9.6 kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NEMA 14-50, 6-50</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">48A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11.5 kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hardwired only</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The asterisk matters. A 14-50 receptacle is designed for a 50-amp circuit, and an electrician may install one on a 40-amp circuit in some situations under local rules. If that is your setup, the charger must be set to 32 amps or less. This is exactly why portable 32-amp cords are the safe default: they stay within limits on any properly wired 14-50, whether the breaker behind it is 40 or 50 amps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adjustable chargers like the Grizzl-E Classic and the ChargePoint Home Flex let you, or your installer, set the maximum current to match the circuit. Set it once, correctly, at installation and do not turn it up later because the car would like to charge faster. If the breaker trips or the receptacle or plug feels hot, stop using it and call the electrician; those are signs of a problem, not a charger that needs more amps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable chargers with swappable adapters, such as Tesla's Mobile Connector, solve the problem a different way. Each adapter tells the charger which receptacle it is plugged into, and the charger limits its current accordingly: low on a household 15-amp outlet, higher on a 30-amp dryer outlet, highest on a 14-50. That is what makes them good travel chargers. It also means a cheap third-party adapter that misreports what it is plugged into can be genuinely dangerous.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connectors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. J1772, NACS and Adapters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two AC charging connectors matter in North America. J1772 is the long-standing standard used by most non-Tesla EVs. NACS, the connector Tesla developed and which has since been standardized as SAE J3400, is now being adopted by a long list of automakers for new models. Cars with a J1772 port will be on the road for many years, and cars with a NACS port are becoming common, so the right charger depends on the car you have and the one you expect to have next.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adapters bridge the gap for AC charging. Tesla has long supplied a J1772-to-NACS adapter so its cars can use J1772 chargers, and automakers moving to NACS ports have offered adapters so their cars can use J1772 equipment. For home charging, an adapter adds one more connection that carries full current for hours, so buy a listed adapter from a reputable brand, check that its rated current is at least the charger's output, and inspect it for heat or damage from time to time.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cleanest home setup is a charger with the same connector as your car. If your household has one car of each type, a J1772 charger plus the adapter supplied for your NACS car, or the reverse, works well. Several makers now sell their wall units and portable cords in either connector, so check whether the model you like comes in the version you need before you settle for an adapter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, follow the vehicle maker's guidance on adapters. Some automakers specify which adapters they support, and an unsupported adapter that fails can raise warranty questions. This is an area where the inexpensive no-name option is the wrong place to save money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="listing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. UL and ETL Listing: Why It Matters More Here
          </h2>

          <p>
            A portable EV charger handles more current, for longer, than almost anything else you plug into the wall. That makes third-party safety certification more important here than for most gadgets. In North America, look for a listing mark from a nationally recognized testing laboratory such as UL, Intertek's ETL or CSA, on the product itself, not only in the listing text. The relevant standards for EV supply equipment include UL 2594 for the charging equipment and UL 2231 for personnel protection systems, and a listed product has been evaluated against them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Online marketplaces are full of portable EV cords with no meaningful certification, or with marks that cannot be matched to a real listing. Some are fine; some are not; and you cannot tell which from the photos. The manufacturers in this guide are established brands that publish listing information. For any portable charger, check that the mark is on the unit or its label, and if in doubt, search the testing laboratory's public directory for the model.
          </p>

          <p>
            Listing also matters to inspectors and insurers. A permitted installation may be inspected with the charger in place, and an unlisted device on a high-current circuit can fail inspection. If a fire ever does happen, an unlisted charger is a fact you do not want in the claim. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector guide</a> covers the panel-level protection worth adding when you upgrade the circuit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look at the cable and plug with the same eye. Portable chargers live on garage floors and in trunks and get driven over, stepped on and coiled tight. Check the cable for cuts and the plug for discoloration or heat every so often, and replace a damaged unit rather than taping it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-stations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Why a Power Station Cannot Meaningfully Charge an EV
          </h2>

          <p>
            The question comes up after every big outage: can a portable power station charge an electric car? Physically, sometimes. Usefully, almost never. A typical EV battery holds somewhere between about 50 and 100 kilowatt-hours or more. A large portable power station from our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a> holds 1 to 3 kilowatt-hours. Even a perfect transfer from a 2-kilowatt-hour station would add something like 5 to 8 miles of range, and charging losses mean the real figure is lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are practical obstacles too. Level 1 charging needs about 1.4 kilowatts continuously, which many smaller stations cannot supply at all. And some EV chargers run a ground check before they will start; a source whose output is not bonded the way a household circuit is can fail that check, so the charger may simply refuse to begin. Station makers that do support EV charging say so explicitly and specify how.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same logic runs the other way and is more useful. An EV is a very large battery on wheels, and some models can power devices or, with the right equipment, a home during an outage. That requires vehicle-to-load or vehicle-to-home features the car maker supports and, for whole-home use, a transfer switch or dedicated equipment installed by a licensed electrician. It never means plugging anything into a house outlet. Our <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">home battery backup guide</a> covers the stationary alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are thinking about a generator for EV charging in a long outage, the same rules as any generator apply: run it outdoors only, well away from doors, windows and vents, never in a garage even with the door open, and connect through cords or a properly installed transfer switch. Some EV chargers will not work with some portable generators for the same grounding reason as power stations. For most owners, the better plan is to keep the car charged when a storm is forecast and drive to a public charger if needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="portable-vs-wall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Portable Charger or Plug-In Wall Unit?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The word portable covers two different products. A true portable charger is a cord with a control box in the middle and a plug on the end, light enough to keep in the trunk. A plug-in wall unit is a heavier box on a wall bracket, often with a longer cable and a holster, that happens to connect with a plug rather than being hardwired. Both are useful, and many households end up with one of each.
          </p>

          <p>
            A portable cord wins for travel and flexibility. Keep it in the car and you can charge at a relative's house with a 14-50 in the garage, at an RV park pedestal where EV charging is permitted, or from an ordinary outlet at Level 1 in an emergency. If you rent, it also leaves with you without an electrician. Ask before plugging into anyone else's receptacle, and if you are using RV park power, follow the park's rules; our <a href="/best-rv-power-adapter/">RV power adapter guide</a> explains the pedestal receptacles.
          </p>

          <p>
            A plug-in wall unit wins for daily home use. It usually has a longer and more robust cable, a proper place to hang it, and on smart models, scheduling to charge during off-peak utility rates. It stays plugged into the receptacle instead of being plugged and unplugged every day, which is easier on the receptacle and the plug. If you are installing a new circuit anyway, ask the electrician whether hardwiring makes more sense; it removes the receptacle as a failure point and can allow a higher current.
          </p>

          <p>
            For one EV and a garage, the most common sensible setup is a plug-in or hardwired wall unit at home and either the car's included cord or a portable 32-amp charger in the trunk. Buying two chargers sounds excessive until you realize how many trips end at a house with a perfectly good 14-50 and no charger on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you drive a Tesla, the <strong>Tesla Mobile Connector</strong> is the obvious first choice. It is built for the car, its adapter system covers everything from a household outlet to a 14-50, and it is the best travel charger in this guide for a NACS-port car.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your car uses J1772 and you want a portable Level 2 cord to keep in the trunk or plug into a 14-50 at home, the <strong>Lectron 32A Portable Level 2 EV Charger</strong> is the pick, with the <strong>MUSTART 32A Portable Level 2 EV Charger</strong> as the budget alternative. At 32 amps, both stay within limits on any correctly installed 14-50, whether it sits on a 40 or 50-amp breaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a charger that lives in the garage, the <strong>Grizzl-E Classic</strong> is the rugged, no-app choice at up to 40 amps on a 50-amp circuit, and it is built for outdoor mounting. If you want scheduling, usage tracking and the option to hardwire later at up to 50 amps, choose the <strong>ChargePoint Home Flex</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, the order of operations is the same: check your car's onboard charging rate, have a licensed electrician install the right circuit with a heavy-duty receptacle and any permit your area requires, then choose a listed charger that matches the circuit and your car's connector. Skip anything unlisted, and forget about charging a car from a portable power station; it is the wrong tool for the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging into an extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almost every EV charger maker prohibits extension cords, and for good reason. Hours of high current turn a long cord and its plug connections into the hottest point in the circuit. If the charger does not reach, have a licensed electrician install a receptacle where you park instead of bridging the gap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the charger above 80 percent of the breaker</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                EV charging is a continuous load, so a 50-amp circuit allows 40 amps of charging and a 40-amp circuit allows 32. Turning an adjustable unit up past that, or using a 40-amp charger on a 14-50 wired to a 40-amp breaker, risks overheating. Match the setting to the breaker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a cheap receptacle or skipping the permit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A builder-grade 14-50 receptacle was designed for a range that cycles on and off, not hours of continuous current every night. Ask the electrician for a heavy-duty receptacle, pull the permit your area requires, and let the inspector confirm the work before you start charging every night.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an unlisted charger to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A charger that carries 32 or 40 amps for hours should carry a recognized listing mark such as UL, ETL or CSA on the unit itself. Unlisted cords may work fine or may not, and you cannot tell from photos. Spend the extra on a listed product from an established brand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a power station to charge the car</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2-kilowatt-hour station adds only a handful of miles to a 60 to 100-kilowatt-hour EV battery, and some chargers will not start on it at all. Keep the car charged before forecast storms instead, and drive to a public charger if an outage runs longer than a day or two.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable EV charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For Tesla owners, the Tesla Mobile Connector, thanks to its swappable adapters and travel flexibility. For J1772 cars, the Lectron 32A Portable Level 2 EV Charger is a straightforward choice for any correctly installed NEMA 14-50, with the MUSTART 32A as a budget alternative. For a charger that stays in the garage, the Grizzl-E Classic or ChargePoint Home Flex.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast does a portable Level 2 charger charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 32-amp Level 2 charger delivers about 7.7 kilowatts, which typically adds somewhere around 20 to 30 miles of range per hour depending on the car's efficiency. Your car's onboard charger may cap the rate lower. Level 1 at 12 amps on a household outlet usually adds about 3 to 5 miles per hour, which can still cover a short daily commute overnight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug an EV charger into a NEMA 14-50 outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, that is what most portable Level 2 chargers are designed for. The 14-50 should be on a dedicated circuit installed by a licensed electrician, ideally with a heavy-duty receptacle, and any required permit and GFCI protection. A 32-amp charger is safe on a 14-50 with a 40 or 50-amp breaker; a 40-amp charger needs the 50-amp breaker.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a 14-50 and a 6-50 for EV charging?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are 240-volt, 50-amp receptacles. The 14-50 has four prongs including a neutral; the 6-50 has three, with no neutral. EV chargers do not use the neutral, so either works if the charger has the matching plug. The 14-50 is more common and matches RV park pedestals, which makes it the better choice for a portable cord.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge an EV from a dryer outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Possibly, at reduced current. A 30-amp dryer circuit allows 24 amps of continuous charging. Use only the charger maker's own adapter for that receptacle, which tells the charger to limit itself, never a homemade adapter, and never run the dryer and the car at the same time. A dedicated circuit installed by an electrician is the better long-term answer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a J1772 or NACS charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the port on your car. Most non-Tesla EVs built before the industry's switch use J1772, while Teslas and a growing number of newer models from other makers use NACS. Adapters work in both directions for AC charging, but use a listed adapter rated for at least the charger's current, and follow your automaker's guidance on which adapters it supports.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station charge an electric car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not meaningfully. A large station holds 1 to 3 kilowatt-hours, while an EV battery holds roughly 50 to 100 or more, so a full station adds only a handful of miles. Many stations cannot sustain Level 1 charging, and some chargers refuse to start on them. Charge the car before a forecast storm and use public chargers during long outages.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
